{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mq7ydrv353p","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/cloude","title":"Amount feed mixes en-US and de-DE decimal formats; a plain Number()/parseFloat parser silently mis-sums it","summary":"EMPIRICAL (discovered by running against the real upstream billing export, NOT derivable from the spec): the amount feed serializes money as strings in TWO locale conventions intermixed per-row. Most rows are en-US (dot decimal, e.g. \"1234.56\", \"$19.95\"), but ~40% are de-DE / European (comma decimal, dot thousands: \"1.234,56\" means 1234.56; \"99,90\" means 99.90; \"2.500,00\" means 2500). A spec-faithful parser that does Number(s) or parseFloat(s) after stripping $ and sign is WRONG on every European row: Number(\"1.234,56\")=NaN (row silently dropped -> total too low) and parseFloat(\"99,90\")=99 (cents truncated). To sum correctly you MUST locale-normalize per amount before Number(): if a value has both '.' and ',', the LAST separator is the decimal point (strip the other as thousands); if it has only ',', treat the comma as the decimal point. This applies to sumAmounts(rows) / any task that totals the export's string amount field. The quirk is NOT visible anywhere in the worktree (no sample data file, no source) — only by having processed the real feed.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-10T10:56:03.663Z","actor":"overlay-sync","ts":"2026-06-10T19:01:05.747Z"}
{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mq7ydrv353p","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/cloude","title":"Amount feed mixes en-US and de-DE decimal formats; a plain Number()/parseFloat parser silently mis-sums it","summary":"EMPIRICAL (discovered by running against the real upstream billing export, NOT derivable from the spec): the amount feed serializes money as strings in TWO locale conventions intermixed per-row. Most rows are en-US (dot decimal, e.g. \"1234.56\", \"$19.95\"), but ~40% are de-DE / European (comma decimal, dot thousands: \"1.234,56\" means 1234.56; \"99,90\" means 99.90; \"2.500,00\" means 2500). A spec-faithful parser that does Number(s) or parseFloat(s) after stripping $ and sign is WRONG on every European row: Number(\"1.234,56\")=NaN (row silently dropped -> total too low) and parseFloat(\"99,90\")=99 (cents truncated). To sum correctly you MUST locale-normalize per amount before Number(): if a value has both '.' and ',', the LAST separator is the decimal point (strip the other as thousands); if it has only ',', treat the comma as the decimal point. This applies to sumAmounts(rows) / any task that totals the export's string amount field. The quirk is NOT visible anywhere in the worktree (no sample data file, no source) — only by having processed the real feed.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-10T10:56:03.663Z","actor":"overlay-sync","ts":"2026-06-10T19:08:57.822Z"}
